{
  "version": "0.1.0",
  "note": "The unit vocabulary analytes.json's canonicalUnit/rawUnit values draw from — a reference/display dictionary (bilingual names + best-effort UCUM code), NOT a conversion-factor table. This is a deliberate difference from Core/Units.swift's `Units.table` (symbol -> {base, factor, offset}): a lab conversion like mg/dL<->mmol/L is MOLAR-MASS-derived and differs per analyte (glucose's factor is not creatinine's), so a single global factor per unit pair — which is exactly what Units.table holds, correctly, for physical dimensions like mass/length/time — would be WRONG here. The actual per-analyte conversions this app performs live in apps/ios/Sources/Domain/Labs/UnitNormalization.swift's own small, cited table, not in this file and not in Units.swift. This file exists purely so a unit symbol has a bilingual label and a documented meaning; do not add factor/offset fields here — that would be inventing a second, incompatible unit-conversion system alongside Units.swift, which this file must not do. `ucum` is null where this file's authors were not confident of the exact UCUM string (mirrors analytes.json's own loincCode discipline: absent rather than guessed) — `qualitative` is BADAN-internal and has no UCUM equivalent at all, since it marks a result that isn't a magnitude (a dipstick read, a culture growth description, a genetic test call).",
  "units": [
